A can supply only 70% of demand and pegged fiscal 2028 growth at approximately 70%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Jensen Huang\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of demand NVIDIA can supply\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"fiscal 2028 growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CEO's statement that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and his ~70% fiscal 2028 growth forecast indicate demand exceeds supply, a demand signal for the Vera Rubin ramp; it does not confirm shipment timing.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-vera-rubin-platform-value\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. says Vera Rubin raises NVIDIA's platform value per gigawatt to $40 billion from Blackwell's $25 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Blackwell)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Blackwell\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Vera Rubin)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Vera Rubin\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Provides an estimate of Vera Rubin's platform value per gigawatt versus Blackwell, informing the economics of the Vera Rubin ramp tracked by the thesis; it does not establish shipment timing.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B40\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion, as cited in the 24/7 Wall St. article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total, up 105.85% year over year; non-GAAP EPS $2.22\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, and the guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue because of geopolitical uncertainty, as reported in the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company outlook; excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's Q3 revenue guidance is stated to exclude China Data Center compute revenue because of geopolitical uncertainty, which bears directly on whether China data-center revenue can resume in fiscal 2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margins to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in fiscal Q4 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected to bottom on memory cost inflation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"recovery as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article cites NVIDIA's risk exposures as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"as cited in the article as a risk exposure\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Guarantee obligations\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as capped, as cited in the article\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as extending to 60 days\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives a stated amount of $108.5 billion of guarantee obligations (described as capped), the kind of credit-support figure clean-1 tracks for disclosure at the next annual report; the article does not say whether it is a disclosed balance or an estimate.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives a stated $279 billion of supply commitments, the purchase/supply obligation quantity clean-2 tracks at the next annual report; the article gives no comparison period or disclosure source.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part4.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"articles\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"TEXT\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"]==6 else \\\"\\\", \\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\"),\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"))\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"])\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  print(json.dumps({k:s.get(k) for k in [\\\"id\\\",\\\"summary\\\",\\\"statement_party\\\",\\\"statement_date\\\",\\\"figures\\\",\\\"links\\\"]},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part4.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"articles\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"TEXT\\\",a[\\\"text\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"]==6 else \\\"\\\", \\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\"),\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"))\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]:\n print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"])\n for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]:\n  print(json.dumps({k:s.get(k) for k in [\\\"id\\\",\\\"summary\\\",\\\"statement_party\\\",\\\"statement_date\\\",\\\"figures\\\",\\\"links\\\"]},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 7, in <module>\n  File \"<string>\", line 7, in <dictcomp>\nAttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'get'\nARTICLE 1 TEXT  QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}] NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'the 45% revenue growth expected for fiscal 2028 before the guide'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'the 70% revenue growth guided for fiscal 2028'}]\nARTICLE 2 TEXT  QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}] NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'about $25 billion a year returned through the dividend'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'the 2,400% increase of the dividend'}, {'figure': 3, 'text': 'the $0.25 quarterly dividend'}]\nARTICLE 3 TEXT  QUESTIONS None NAMED []\nARTICLE 6 TEXT NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms. QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}] NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'fiscal 2028 growth of approximately 70%'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand'}]\nARTICLE 7 TEXT  QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}] NAMED []\nARTICLE 9 TEXT  QUESTIONS None NAMED []\nANSWER B31 ARTICLE 1\n{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue up 117% to $89 billion, as reported by barchart.com.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total, more than doubled year over year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"data center revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"segment, up 117% year over year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, implying 89% growth, as reported by barchart.com.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\", \"value\": \"approximately $108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company revenue, implying 89% growth\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, far above the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled, as reported by barchart.com.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company revenue growth\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"45%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"sell-side consensus expectation before the company's guidance\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"barchart.com reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\", \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"roughly 25%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"article's expectation for company revenue\", \"party\": \"barchart.com\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"company-backed financing funded off NVIDIA's balance sheet\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"potentially $175 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"article's expectation; potential amount, not a reported balance\", \"party\": \"barchart.com\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of revenue tied to customers receiving NVIDIA-backed financing (about 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue, potentially $175 billion off its balance sheet), informing the amount of credit support and revenue backstops the thesis tracks at the next annual report.\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"barchart.com states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduce credit risk not captured by traditional valuation metrics.\", \"statement_party\": null, \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"off-balance-sheet guarantees\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"exceeding $100 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI; stated as introducing credit risk\", \"party\": null}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Reports a figure of more than $100 billion of off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to an OpenAI-related project, directly bearing on the guarantee and credit-support amount the thesis tracks at the next annual report.\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"e8\", \"summary\": \"barchart.com reports that analysts' earnings estimates for NVIDIA were revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029, alongside 45 of 50 firms rating the stock \\\"Strong Buy\\\".\", \"statement_party\": \"Sell-side analyst consensus\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"earnings per share estimate\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\", \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"analyst consensus, revised up from $8.50 over the past nine months\", \"party\": \"Sell-side analyst consensus\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\nANSWER B32 ARTICLE 7\n{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total revenue for the quarter, as cited by Trefis.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"data center revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Data center segment revenue for the quarter; no accounting basis stated\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"total revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"$96 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Company total revenue for the quarter; no accounting basis stated\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which Trefis calculates is about 12% above the fiscal Q2 total.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Company total revenue; management guidance with a ±2% band\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and it reset its margin expectations, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Company gross margin for the quarter; no accounting basis stated\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"74%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Company gross margin; management guidance, down from 75% in fiscal Q2\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance (expected bottom)\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\", \"value\": \"71% to 72%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Company gross margin; management expectation that margin bottoms in fiscal Q4\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}\n{\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Cumulative amount described as invested; no period or accounting basis stated\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Reports the equity-investment side of the comparison the thesis tracks (guarantees and credit support versus equity investments in AI labs), giving a nearly $50 billion cumulative investment figure, though it does not quantify any guarantee or backstop.\"}]}\n{\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028, a portion that would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\", \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Management expectation; NVIDIA-funded AI lab customers as a share of the company's business\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Quantifies how much of NVIDIA's business management expects to come from AI labs it funds, which is the exposure the thesis tracks alongside the credit support, revenue backstops and equity investments NVIDIA discloses.\"}]}\nANSWER B33 ARTICLE 9\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json;p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part4.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"articles\\\"]:\n if a[\\\"article\\\"] in (7,9): print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"publication_date\\\"],a[\\\"text\\\"])\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"][2:]: print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",json.dumps(a,ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json;p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-part4.json\\\"));\nfor a in p[\\\"articles\\\"]:\n if a[\\\"article\\\"] in (7,9): print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"publication_date\\\"],a[\\\"text\\\"])\nfor a in p[\\\"answers\\\"][2:]: print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",json.dumps(a,ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 7 2026-09-25 What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\nHow To Act On TXN?\nARTICLE 9 2026-09-27 (Bloomberg) -- The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\nEllison Pledges $9.2 Billion More in Oracle Shares for Loans\nMcDonald's Selloff Hits 30% as Big Mac Inflation Spurs Pushback\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was released this month, including how many of them and what they would use the product for, the report said, adding that the ministry told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\nNvidia has faced export restrictions from the US as well as curbs from Beijing over the sale of its chips to China, due to a web of bilateral tensions ranging from security to competition on artificial intelligence.\nThe US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\nGLP-1 Users Are Furious After Employers Drop Insurance Benefits\nThis $2 Billion Company Dresses HBO's The Pitt Cast and Real Doctors Too\nANSWER {\"answer\": \"B33\", \"article\": 9, \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"}\nANSWER {\"answer\": \"B34\", \"article\": 2, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"In May 2026 Nvidia told shareholders it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase of 2,400% by the Morningstar author's calculation. The author estimates that roughly $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders through the dividend, which would make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\", \"passages\": [\"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\", \"But in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders. That sum will make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": \"May 2026 (item: \\\"In May\\\")\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\", \"period\": \"quarterly, declared May 2026\", \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"raised from $0.01 per share\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\", \"period\": \"before May 2026\", \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"annual dividend payout to shareholders\", \"period\": \"per year\", \"value\": \"$25 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Morningstar author's calculation (\\\"Something like\\\")\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration, Morningstar reported, adding that most of Nvidia's distributions to shareholders come as share repurchases rather than dividends.\", \"passages\": [\"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration. The company’s preference when it comes to cash allocations is clear in the exhibit below.\", \"“Most of Nvidia’s distributions to shareholders come in the form of share repurchases,” writes Colello.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": \"May 2026 (announced at the time of the dividend declaration)\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"share buyback program\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$80 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"announced at the time of the dividend declaration\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Morningstar cites Nvidia's own forecast of 70% year-on-year revenue growth, mentioned among the year's news flow and again in noting that the large dividend increase does not fit a claim that Nvidia is entering a slower-growth phase. The item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers.\", \"passages\": [\"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\", \"That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"year-on-year; Nvidia's own forecast, period not specified in the item\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}\nANSWER {\"answer\": \"B35\", \"article\": 3, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA Corporation reported in its Aug. 26 press release (as cited by Zacks) that for its fiscal second quarter of 2027 Data Center segment revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, consolidated revenue was $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, and non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5% a year earlier.\", \"passages\": [\"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release.\", \"Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\", \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\", \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenues\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenue growth, year over year\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"117%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenues\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"consolidated\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenue growth, year over year\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"106%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"consolidated\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"}, {\"n\": 5, \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided revenue for its fiscal third quarter of 2027 to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and said it expects profitability to remain strong; at the midpoint of that guidance revenue would rise 12% sequentially, according to Zacks' account of the company's outlook.\", \"passages\": [\"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027.\", \"At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\", \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenues guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint\", \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"12%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"at the midpoint of guidance; sequential\", \"party\": \"Zacks\"}]}]}\nANSWER {\"answer\": \"B36\", \"article\": 9, \"statements_with_figures\": \"none kept\"}\nANSWER {\"answer\": \"B37\", \"article\": 3, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA, in its Aug. 26 press release, reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 consolidated revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, with Data Center segment revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially; non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5% a year earlier.\", \"passages\": [\"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\", \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenues\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"consolidated; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenues\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue growth, year over year\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"117%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Data Center segment, year over year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; versus 72.5% a year ago\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenues to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which at the midpoint the article says would be a 12% sequential rise; the company said it further expects profitability to remain strong.\", \"passages\": [\"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenues (guidance)\", \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint\", \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\", \"value\": \"12%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"sequential rise at the midpoint of guidance; article's calculation\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}]}]}\nANSWER {\"answer\": \"B38\", \"article\": 6, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29% and Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion, per 24/7 Wall St.'s buy note.\", \"passages\": [\"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"105.85%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$2.22\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St.\", \"passages\": [\"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang told investors the company can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\", \"passages\": [\"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"approximately 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"pegged at approximately 70%\", \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. cites NVIDIA's Vera Rubin production ramp as one of three catalysts for its buy call, saying Vera Rubin lifts platform value per gigawatt to $40 billion from Blackwell's $25 billion and citing management's description of the ramp as the fastest in company history; the item gives no shipment date or volume.\", \"passages\": [\"Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\", \"Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Vera Rubin)\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$40 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"platform value per gigawatt, versus Blackwell\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Blackwell)\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$25 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Blackwell comparison basis cited for Vera Rubin\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Reports that the Vera Rubin ramp is under way, with management calling it the fastest in company history, which bears on whether Vera Rubin reaches volume customer shipments in fiscal 2027; the item gives no shipment or revenue-recognition date, so it does not establish the milestone.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e7\", \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists supply commitments of $279 billion among the real risks in the NVIDIA case; the item does not say on what date or in which filing that amount was reported.\", \"passages\": [\"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"], \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"supply commitments\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$279 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"presented as an existing risk; source and as-of date of the figure not stated in the item\", \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-2\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Gives a figure for NVIDIA's supply commitments ($279 billion) that the thesis tracks against the purchase and supply commitment amount disclosed in the next Form 10-K; the item gives no as-of date, so it cannot be tied to a specific reporting period.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e8\", \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among the real risks in the NVIDIA case; the item gives no as-of date or source for the figure.\", \"passages\": [\"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"], \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations (capped)\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"capped; source and as-of date not stated in the item\", \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"States a $108.5 billion cap on NVIDIA's guarantee obligations, the exact quantity the thesis tracks at the next annual report; the item does not say whether this reflects disclosed guarantees, credit support for AI labs and cloud partners, or the analyst's own aggregation.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e9\", \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. says NVIDIA's days sales outstanding are extending to 60 days and lists this among the real risks in the case.\", \"passages\": [\"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"], \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"60 days\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"described as extending\", \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e13\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margins to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in fiscal Q4 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\", \"passages\": [\"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"71% to 72%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"expected to bottom, on memory cost inflation\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"72% to 73%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"recovery as volumes scale\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}\nANSWER {\"answer\": \"B39\", \"article\": 6, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"ev-q2-fy27-results\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion.\", \"passages\": [\"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"105.85%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"year over year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$2.22\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Data Center\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-q3-fy27-guidance-china-exclusion\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion; the Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, citing geopolitical uncertainty.\", \"passages\": [\"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\", \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"excludes China Data Center compute revenue\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-3\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"The guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, showing China data-center sales are not in the Q3 outlook and bearing on whether NVIDIA can resume China sales in FY2027.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-gross-margin-guidance\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71%–72% in Q4 fiscal 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72%–73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\", \"passages\": [\"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"Q4\", \"value\": \"71% to 72%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"on memory cost inflation\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"72% to 73%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"as volumes scale\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-supply-guarantees-dso\", \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists NVIDIA's risk exposures as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\", \"passages\": [\"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"supply commitments\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$279 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"capped at\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"60 days\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"extending to\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-2\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Reports $279 billion of supply commitments, directly the purchase/supply commitment amount clean-2 tracks at the next annual report.\"}, {\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Reports guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, directly the credit-support/guarantee amount clean-1 tracks at the next annual report.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-jensen-supply-demand-growth\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and pegged fiscal 2028 growth at approximately 70%.\", \"passages\": [\"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"], \"statement_party\": \"Jensen Huang\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"share of demand NVIDIA can supply\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"fiscal 2028 growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"approximately 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"The CEO's statement that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and his ~70% fiscal 2028 growth forecast indicate demand exceeds supply, a demand signal for the Vera Rubin ramp; it does not confirm shipment timing.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-vera-rubin-platform-value\", \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. says Vera Rubin raises NVIDIA's platform value per gigawatt to $40 billion from Blackwell's $25 billion.\", \"passages\": [\"Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\"], \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\", \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-23\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Blackwell)\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$25 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Blackwell\", \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Vera Rubin)\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$40 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Vera Rubin\", \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Provides an estimate of Vera Rubin's platform value per gigawatt versus Blackwell, informing the economics of the Vera Rubin ramp tracked by the thesis; it does not establish shipment timing.\"}]}]}\nANSWER {\"answer\": \"B40\", \"article\": 6, \"statements_with_figures\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion, as cited in the 24/7 Wall St. article.\", \"passages\": [\"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total, up 105.85% year over year; non-GAAP EPS $2.22\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$2.22\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\", \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"segment\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, and the guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue because of geopolitical uncertainty, as reported in the article.\", \"passages\": [\"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\", \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company outlook; excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-3\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Management's Q3 revenue guidance is stated to exclude China Data Center compute revenue because of geopolitical uncertainty, which bears directly on whether China data-center revenue can resume in fiscal 2027.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margins to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in fiscal Q4 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale, according to the article.\", \"passages\": [\"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Gross margin\", \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\", \"value\": \"71% to 72%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"expected to bottom on memory cost inflation\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Gross margin\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"72% to 73%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"recovery as volumes scale\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"The article cites NVIDIA's risk exposures as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\", \"passages\": [\"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"], \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"statement_date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Supply commitments\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$279 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"as cited in the article as a risk exposure\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Guarantee obligations\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"described as capped, as cited in the article\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"Days sales outstanding (DSO)\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"60 days\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"described as extending to 60 days\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Gives a stated amount of $108.5 billion of guarantee obligations (described as capped), the kind of credit-support figure clean-1 tracks for disclosure at the next annual report; the article does not say whether it is a disclosed balance or an estimate.\"}, {\"question\": \"clean-2\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Gives a stated $279 billion of supply commitments, the purchase/supply obligation quantity clean-2 tracks at the next annual report; the article gives no comparison period or disclosure source.\"}]}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
